data.content:
## Migration: `TimedExecutor` → .NET 8

**Pattern identified:** Timeout enforcement + ThreadAbortException as control flow + ResetAbort

### Why it breaks

`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 6+. `Thread.ResetAbort()` and `ThreadAbortException` are also gone. You must switch to **cooperative cancellation**.

### Migrated code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // Task.WaitAsync throws OperationCanceledException on timeout
            return task.WaitAsync(cts.Token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
        catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerExceptions.Count == 1)
        {
            throw new AggregateException(ex.InnerException!);
        }
    }
}
```

### If `work` must actually *stop* (not just be abandoned)

The code above **abandons** the task on timeout — the work keeps running in the background. To truly stop it, the work must cooperate:

```csharp
public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
    var token = cts.Token;

    try
    {
        var task = Task.Run(() => work(token), token);
        return task.WaitAsync(token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
    }
    catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        throw new TimeoutException(
            $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
    }
}

// Caller must check the token:
executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(token =>
{
    foreach (var item in items)
    {
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // checkpoint
        Process(item);
    }
    return result;
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30));
```

### Key pitfalls for this migration

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---------|--------|
| **Work doesn't actually stop** | Unlike `Thread.Abort`, cancellation is cooperative. If `work` never checks the token, it runs to completion in the background even after timeout. |
| **Leaking `CancellationTokenSource`** | Always `using`/dispose it — it holds timer resources when created with a timeout. |
| **`Thread.Sleep` inside work** | Won't respond to cancellation. Replace with `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(ms)`. |
| **Swallowing `OperationCanceledException`** | Only catch it at the top orchestration point (here, to convert to `TimeoutException`). Let it propagate everywhere else. |
| **Blocking I/O in work** | Sync calls like `socket.Read()` won't cancel. Use async overloads that accept `CancellationToken`. |

### What was removed and why

| Old API | Replacement | Reason |
|---------|-------------|--------|
| `Thread.Abort()` | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` (implicit via timeout) | `SYSLIB0006` / `PlatformNotSupportedException` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` | New exception type for cooperative cancellation |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Removed entirely | No abort to reset; check `token.IsCancellationRequested` if you want to continue |
| Manual `new Thread(...)` | `Task.Run` | Thread pool–friendly; pairs naturally with `CancellationToken` |

The **fundamental shift**: .NET Framework let you forcibly kill a thread from outside. Modern .NET requires the running code to *volunteer* to stop. Design checkpoint frequency based on your acceptable cancellation latency.
